    "cards": [
      {
        "icon": "❤️",
        "title": "If Your Heart Talks, Stop",
        "body": "<p>This hasn't changed, and it matters more now that the weights go up. <strong>Stop the session, sit down and don't drive</strong> if you get any of these:</p><ul><li>Pain, pressure or tightness in your chest</li><li>A racing, thumping or irregular heartbeat, even resting between sets</li><li>Feeling light-headed, your vision going grey, or like you might pass out</li><li>Breathing much harder than the work should make you</li><li>New pain going into your jaw, neck or arm</li><li>A cold sweat</li></ul><p>If any of that happens, <strong>see a doctor the same day</strong> and message me before your next session. I'd rather you stop ten times for nothing than push through once.</p><p>There are still no heart rate targets anywhere in your program. Until I know what your doctor says and whether you take any medication, your heart rate isn't a number I can read for you.</p>",
        "tags": [
          "pain-ladder"
        ]
      },
      {
        "icon": "🩺",
        "title": "See Your Doctor This Block",
        "body": "<p>You haven't seen your doctor yet, so nothing around your heart has changed in your program. <strong>Please go before this block ends, by 7 November.</strong></p><p>Here's why. Right now I'm coaching your heart from one sentence, so every set stays at RPE 7, and some machines and anything heavy off the floor stay out. <strong>The doctor's answers decide how hard we can go from here</strong>, and they could open up a lot for you.</p><p>Ask for these four things in writing and send me a photo:</p><ul><li>What the condition is called</li><li>Whether you're cleared for weight training</li><li>Any blood pressure or heart rate limit</li><li>Whether you're on any medication</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "icon": "🛑",
        "title": "When Something Hurts",
        "body": "<p>Your elbow is quiet and your knee took more weight every week last block. Here's how we keep it that way. <strong>Normal and fine:</strong> muscles burning in a set, feeling tired after, stiff the next morning. Keep training.</p><p><strong>Not fine:</strong> sharp pain, anything shooting down your arm or leg, pins and needles, or pain that builds through a set.</p><ul><li><strong>Mild and fading:</strong> finish the set and write it in your log</li><li><strong>Building, or changing how you move:</strong> stop that exercise, use the swap on the card, message me</li><li><strong>Sharp, shooting, numb or just weird:</strong> stop the session and message me before the next one</li></ul><p><strong>Telling me gets you a change, never a ban.</strong> Knee sore or puffier the next morning: the mats under your lunge go back up one level and you tell me. Elbow worse the next morning: one dumbbell lighter on the wrist work and tell me that day. The mats only come down after three clean sessions in a row, and only I move them.</p>",
        "tags": [
          "pain-ladder",
          "modification-menu"
        ]
      },
      {
        "icon": "🎥",
        "title": "Film These Two, Every Week",
        "body": "<p>Same as last block, and you've been great at it. <strong>Film your top set of the Single-Leg Dumbbell Hip Thrust on Day 1 and the Single-Arm Chest-Supported Dumbbell Row on Day 3, every week.</strong></p><p>Side-on, sound on, sent on WhatsApp the same day. I'm watching your breathing on the 3 + 3 as much as your form, so the first week's videos matter most.</p><p>Your form was good in every video last block, which is why these two can get heavier now. <strong>If the videos stop, the weight stops moving.</strong></p>",
        "tags": [
          "film"
        ]
      },
      {
        "icon": "🧱",
        "title": "How 3 + 3 Works",
        "body": "<p>Your two main lifts are built differently this block. <strong>Do 3 reps, rest the weight and take a slow breath for about 15 seconds, then do 3 more.</strong> That's one set. Then the other side.</p><p>The break lets you use more weight while every rep stays clean and every rep still breathes out. <strong>The RPE is for the whole set:</strong> after the second 3, how many more could you have done? RPE 7 means three more.</p><p>Start near your top set from last block. If the second 3 felt easier than RPE 7, go up a little next set. If it felt like an 8 or a 9, <strong>write that number down</strong> and take some weight off. Taking weight off when a set runs hot is the right call.</p>",
        "tags": [
          "explainer"
        ]
      },
      {
        "icon": "🏆",
        "title": "Five Weeks Of Real Numbers",
        "body": "<p>Steel Breath was about finding your real numbers, and you found them. <strong>Your hip thrust went from 7.5 to 12.5 kg and your leg curl from 45 to 59 kg, both at the same RPE 7.</strong></p><p>Your row reached 15 kg at RPE 7 and your form was good in every video. On the day you had tennis you did the first lift only, which is exactly the rule.</p><p><strong>And your elbow is quiet.</strong> The wrist work did its job, so it keeps going this block and the weight on it can go up again.</p>",
        "tags": [
          "win",
          "close-loop"
        ]
      },
      {
